      "disposition": "minor_weakness",
      "impact": "low",
      "exploitability": "plausible",
      "confidence": "medium",
      "risk_exposure": "not_demonstrated",
      "recommended_risk": "low",
      "technical_explanation": "The scanner flags co-location, not a data flow. All network retrieval in the supplied excerpt targets the host application's same-origin endpoints for the extension's own persistence (upload, delete, and read of its per-character JSON records) and, separately, the user-selected Connection Manager profile used to request translations of selected card fields. Responses are parsed as JSON and applied as data; the excerpt contains no eval, Function-constructor, or script-execution path fed by network content, and the likely flagged sinks are dynamic innerHTML construction plus a scheduled callback receiving locally generated strings. The persistence behavior is disclosed in the project documentation and matches its stated purpose. Residual weakness: dialog and panel HTML interpolates dynamic values such as the character card name and profile display name without escaping, so crafted card metadata could inject markup into the confirmation dialog if upstream sanitization is absent. Render code beyond the truncated excerpt was not available for review, so an end-to-end exploit chain is not demonstrated.",
      "layman_explanation": "This extension only talks to two kinds of places: the roleplay app's own storage, where it saves and reloads its translation notes, and the translation service the user explicitly picks, which translates the selected card fields. Both are advertised in its documentation and gated by a confirmation dialog. The reviewed code never downloads something and then runs it, so the scanner's 'download then execute' warning does not hold up here. One small hygiene gap remains: text such as a character's name is pasted into popup windows without first defusing embedded HTML, so a maliciously crafted card",
      "developer_action": "No urgent change is warranted for the flagged correlation. As low-cost hardening, HTML-escape every dynamic value (character name, profile name, and any stored translation or memo text) before interpolating it into popup or panel markup, or build those nodes with text-based DOM setters, so crafted card metadata can never inject markup.",
      "locations": [
        {
